This analysis will implement the game Garbage in R and determine if there are any game-winning strategies I may employ regarding wild cards. 

## Introduction to Garbage

Garbage, also known as Trash, is a card game traditionally played by two people and one standard deck of 52 playing cards. The dealer deals 10 face down cards to each player, which are then placed side by side in a line 10 cards long. On their turn, the player draws from the deck to replace one of the correspondingly numbered cards face down in front of them. A 3 will be placed in the third slot from the left, a 6 in the 6th, and so on up to the 10th and final slot. Once the player draws a playable card, they place it face up in the appropriate slot and look at the card that was in its place. If that card is playable, they place it face up in the correct slot and look at the next card. This continues until the player has a non-playable card, which is to say any card with a number that’s already face up, or a face card that’s not wild (Kings are wild). The player discards their non-playable card and the next player draws (from the deck or the face-up discard) to continue. Once a player has flipped all their cards face up, they win that round and the next begins. Each round, players are dealt 10 cards, minus the number of rounds they have won. So the player will first have to turn over 10 cards, then 9, then 8 to win the round. Once the player gets down to only one card, they have a chance to win the game by flipping an Ace before the other player can end the round. In other words, Garbage takes a minimum of 10 rounds and a maximum of 19 (if players switch back and forth winning each round).

## Simulation Objectives

I have been fascinated by this game for a long time because it is seemingly very simple, and yet I find it so often impossible to determine what will happen next. Simply trying to calculate the probability of drawing a card I need is obstructed by a number of face-down cards that may or may not contain the numbers in question, and there are many variations to game play that could affect my chances of winning. So my objective is to simulate Garbage in R in order to permute different iterations of the game. By so doing, I hope to find insights to help me win the next game of Garbage I play. 

Therefore, we attempt to create a model that, although it cannot be represented succinctly with only one equation or a computer diagram, is still effective in predicting one particular thing: who will win a game of Garbage.

## Converting a card game to a computer simulation

Unlike human players who can comprehend, synthesize, and make creative decisions based on complex information, computers are quite bad at creatively interpreting vague stimuli. As a result, I must translate the "*way that the game is played*" into a set of strict rules and logical evaluations that allow the algorithm to play the same way a person might. The only difference will be that this computer can be given a set of instructions (strategies, hand sizes, players, etc.) and then play the game itself, returning the winner of the game into a vector which I can analyze in various ways. 

### Functional Style and Instantiaion
In order to implement Garbage, we will adopt a functional style that passes and modifies a "gamestate" object. I originally considered uisng edit-in-place methods like are used in the R6 OOP framework for R. However, I tend to find that R is, in general, much more suited to functional programming than the object-oriented equivalents, and utilizing a system like R6 is only warranted when a functional approach will not succeed at all or be incredibly difficult to implement. As I did not predict that either of those cases would be true, I eventually stuck to a functional style and created a set of functions which first create and then pass around a gamestate object, which contains the deck, discard, active card, player hands and flippedness, and a pointer that stops the turn looper when a player wins that round. Here are the instantiation functions, including the function to deal the specified amount of cards to a given subset of the players. 

