## Simulation Results
Now that we have implemented Garbage, it is time to do some simulating and see the results! The crucial step here is to define the way we want the "player" to play, and then repeat those circumstances over and over until we begin to see a proportion which approaches the true proportion of successes (round victories) when the player is under those conditions. We will iterate 5000 times for each condition (pair of hand sizes), which means each strategy we analyze will contain 500,000 observed rounds of Garbage. To prevent this document from taking 100 years to render each time, I performed these tests and then saved the results in several .csv files. 

Here is the data from a run where the first player always plays their wild card in the last possible slot (the highest face down number):

```{r}

last_start_rand_props <- rio::import("project_data/pl1_last_start_rand_props.csv")

knitr::kable(last_start_rand_props)


```

Note that the proportion of wins for player one under these conditions increase from bottom left to top right, this would indicate that having fewer cards than your opponent increases your chances of winning the round. However, also examine the diagonal, where both players have the same number of cards. Nearly all of these percentages are greater than 0.5; which doesn't seem likely. After, all, if both computer players use the same strategy and the cards are randomized, then over 10,000 games we would expect the proportion of wins for computer 1 vs. computer 2 to approach exactly 0.5. However, this does not appear to be the case. What could be causeing this behavior?
The only clear difference between player 1 and player 2 is that, because of the initial way I wrote the code, player 1 always goes first unless you specify a starting player. Therefore, could these greater than 50% chance to win the game proportions be a result of who got to go first?

## Considering the First Player

Because it seems that the first player had an advantage in those matrices, I also consider whether going first actually increases your chances of winning the round. To do so, I ran $10,000$ runs each under the following conditions:


| Player Condition   | Hand Size | Pl_1 Win Proportion |
|--------------------|-------|-------|
| Random Starter     | 10, 10| 0.4957|
| Random Starter     | 1, 1  | 0.5001|
| Player 1 Starts    | 10, 10| 0.5485|
| Player 1 Starts    | 1, 1. | 0.6144|
| Player 1 Starts (L)| 10, 10| 0.5366|

As we can see, there is a slight difference in who wins the round based on who goes first. After 10,000 runs, we would expect (as we see n the first two) the proportion to be very close to 0.5, which would mean that going first doesn't affect your chances of winning. However, for those games where player 1 went first every time, the proportions are higher than 0.5, with the (1,1) hand size reporting that player 1 wins almost 2/3 of the time, as long as they go first.

## Conclusion and Future Research

While this analysis successfully implemented the basic version of the game Garbage, there are several game variants I would like to investigate. For instance, how do win probabilities change when playing with 3 or more players instead of 2? Or would the benefit of going first go away if the loser got an extra turn after the round winner and could also turn all of their cards over? This would mean that it is possible to tie instead of winning outright; another potential outcome and an interesting question to consider in the future. 

While I did not use a lot of the probability modeling skills I learned from this class (nor from MATH 423) in completing this project, I still feel that it was very interesting from a probabilistic standpoint. For instance, it was pointed out during a live presentation of these findings that the win ratios accompanying each hand size increase from left to right and then peak around 7 or 8 cards (for player 2), before descending slightly again. This is a very intriguing result, and may possibly be due to the interaction between the number of cards that need to be turned over and the number of "junk" cards in the deck, which increases as your hand size decreases. I would like to investigate that further.


































